out the rest of super  thereafter  despite being shown to train alongside  Vegeta and we store the remainder of the  series realistically this advice should  conflict with Goku's emotional state and  ultimately culminate in him accepting  this shortcoming and adopting a better  way of life but then that would be  contradicting Goku's character type Goku  if you remember already has the truth  he's a flat character AK and therefore  gory emotionally doesn't come easily to  him so nothing happens Dragon Ball Z his  workaround for this was to have Goku  impress his mentors or simply not show  his training at all and during these  times others would either be by his side  in these efforts to provide the  necessary struggle or occupying an  adjacent part of the story like piccolo  and Gohan did with in this instance in  Dragon Ball super they tried to give  Goku an emotional fault alongside Vegeta  in Goku's case it's flat out forgotten  about and in the case of Aegina it just  suddenly happens without any gradual  introspection or struggle on bejeezus  behalf specifically concerning this  inability to relax now it could be  argued that we suppose in a no-win  scenario with this particular plotline  it sort of existed to create the twist  ending for the resurrection F arc and  nothing more and I think that's a fair  way to look at it but that still doesn't  mean that least did a good job  the Warriors didn't struggle emotionally  to learn anything and as a result we  never got any cathodic moments due to  these lessons we Swiss espousing which  brings me to my final critique of his  character the exposition in John  troubies book the anatomy of story he  writes you never want to create  characters that sound like a mouthpiece  for your ideas good writers express  their moral vision slow and subtly  primarily through story structure and  the way the hero deals with a particular  situation and the Falla character is  there for is to monologue exposition in  obvious ways or